<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prague</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prague is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and historically of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. In the late nineteenth century it was one of the most economically dynamic cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>. It was also a centre of Czech national life within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prague.jpg" alt="Prague" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moyan Brenn from Italy · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prague_(6365119737).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1848–1849 the army saved the monarchy: Field Marshal Joseph Radetzky defeated Piedmont-Sardinia at Custoza in 1848 and Novara in 1849, and Prince Alfred Windischgrätz retook <b>Prague</b> and Vienna.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Total output (GDP) roughly doubled between 1870 and 1913, with growth concentrated in Vienna, Budapest and <b>Prague</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>On 18 October 1918 Masaryk issued a declaration of independence in Washington, and on 28 October the National Committee in <b>Prague</b> proclaimed the new state as Habsburg authority dissolved.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Maximilian I built a disciplined standing force, founded the Catholic League in 1609 and, with its general Count Tilly, shared in the Catholic victory at the Battle of White Mountain near <b>Prague</b> in 1620.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Heidelberg was the cultural centre: Rupert I founded its university in 1386, the third in the Empire after <b>Prague</b> and Vienna, and the electors' library, the Bibliotheca Palatina, became one of the finest collections in Europe in the sixteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Vienna, <b>Prague</b> and later Pest grew as court, administrative and commercial centres, and Charles VI declared Trieste and Fiume free ports in 1719 to open an outlet to the Adriatic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
